#
# Without this the controller enumerates as /dev/ttyACMn owned by root:dialout, so the driver only works
# for users who have been added to the dialout group.
#
# This file deliberately creates no device name of its own. ScopeLink currently uses STMicroelectronics'
# generic Virtual COM Port identifiers, 0483:5740, which every STM32 device running the stock USB CDC
# stack also reports - Nucleo boards, other vendors' controllers, home-built focusers - and the firmware
# sets no product string either: a generation 3 unit reports ST's default, "STM32 Virtual ComPort in FS
# Mode". Nothing in the descriptors distinguishes a ScopeLink from any other stock-stack STM32 device, so
# a SYMLINK+= here would be handing the name "scopelink" to hardware that is not one.
#
# A product identifier of our own has been requested from STMicroelectronics. Once it is assigned the
# match below becomes exact, and a symlink can be added with it.
#
# Until then, the stable per-unit name to point an Ekos profile at is the one systemd already derives
# from the USB serial number:
#
#     /dev/serial/by-id/usb-STMicroelectronics_STM32_Virtual_ComPort_in_FS_Mode_205E35793630-if00
#
# Matching broadly for access rights is harmless: it grants the user at the console access to a serial
# device they plugged in themselves, which is what a desktop distribution does for CDC devices anyway.

SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="0483", ATTRS{idProduct}=="5740", MODE="0660", GROUP="dialout", TAG+="uaccess"
